<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nehesy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 1705 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nehesy was a king of the Fourteenth Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, which ruled the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-intermediate-period">Second Intermediate Period</a>, and is the best-attested ruler of that line. His name means the Nubian. He reigned briefly around 1705 BCE, and monuments bearing his name have been found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Avaris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-el-muqdam">Tell el-Muqdam</a>, where he is described as beloved of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seth">Seth</a>, lord of Avaris.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nehesy.jpg" alt="Nehesy" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Auguste Mariette &amp;amp; Gaston Maspero (eds.) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Obelisk_Aasehre_Mariette.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
